= [Multi-Dimensional Modeling LTT] Analyze Hierarchical Workout History Structures in a Gym Tracker System
:Author: Jerac Duran

== Objective

Examine how workout history data forms a multi-dimensional domain model through relationships between users, workouts, exercises, sets, reps, and temporal records.

== Description

This lecture explores the concept of multi-dimensional domain modeling using the workout history system of a gym tracker application.
The presentation analyzes how workout data is structured through interconnected entities such as users, workout sessions, exercises, and exercise performance records.

The lecture discusses why workout history cannot be represented as a simple linear structure: workouts contain multiple exercises, and each exercise record contains additional attributes such as sets, reps, weight, and timestamps.

The topic connects these relationships to concepts from the "Domain Attributes" lecture, including dimensions, structured phenomena, recursive relationships, and temporal modeling.

== Database Entities Used

* `workout_sessions`
* `workout_exercises`
* `exercises`
* `goals_feedback`
* `session_comparison`

== Multi-Dimensional Structure of Workout History

The workout history system is multi-dimensional because multiple entities interact simultaneously across several layers of the domain.

The system contains relationships between:

* Users
* Workout sessions
* Exercises
* Sets and reps (per exercise record)
* Weight values
* Timestamps
* Progress records

A single workout session may contain multiple exercises, and each exercise record within that session may carry different sets, reps, and weight values.
No single value or flat table can represent this structure fully, which is what makes the system multi-dimensional.

== Hierarchical Modeling

Workout history is hierarchical rather than purely linear because each entity depends on the one above it in the structure.

.Workout History Hierarchy
[source]
----
auth.users (user_id)
└── workout_sessions (user_id FK → auth.users)
    └── workout_exercises (workout_id FK → workout_sessions)
        └── exercises (exercise_id FK → workout_exercises)
            ├── sets
            ├── reps
            └── weight
----

Users are the root of this hierarchy, but only through `workout_sessions`.
The `workout_exercises` and `exercises` tables have no direct `user_id` column — a user's exercise records are only reachable by traversing through `workout_sessions` first.
This means the hierarchy is strictly top-down: to identify which user performed a given exercise, you must join `workout_exercises` → `workout_sessions` → `auth.users`.

Each level of the hierarchy adds a new dimension to the domain model, and no level can be fully interpreted without its parent.

== Structured Domain Phenomena

The system demonstrates *structured domain phenomena* because workout data cannot be modeled using a single atomic value — it requires a network of related entities.

Examples:

* `workout_sessions` connects `auth.users` with time-bounded workout events via `user_id`
* `workout_exercises` connects each workout session with specific exercises and their performance metrics (`sets`, `reps`, `weight`) via foreign keys to both `workout_sessions` and `exercises`
* `exercises` defines the exercise catalog, providing names and categories referenced by `workout_exercises`
* `goals_feedback` stores user-level goal and feedback records scoped to `user_id`; it has no foreign key to `workout_sessions`, so it tracks progress at the user level independently of individual sessions

These interconnected relationships create a structured and highly dimensional system where meaning depends on the relationships between entities, not just the values stored within them.

== Temporal and Sequential Relationships

Workout history is time-dependent in addition to being structurally complex.

Examples include:

* Workout sessions recorded using `created_at` timestamps, establishing chronological order
* Progress tracking across different `period_date` values in `goals_feedback`
* Session-over-session comparisons enabled by the `session_comparison` entity

The `session_comparison` entity demonstrates how workout history can be analyzed across multiple temporal states — for example, comparing a user's performance in the current session against a previous one.
This temporal dimension adds a sequential axis to the already multi-dimensional domain model.

== Relationship to Domain Attributes Lecture

This topic applies the following concepts discussed in the lecture:

* *Dimensions* — workout data spans user, session, exercise, performance, and time dimensions simultaneously
* *Structured domain phenomena* — the domain cannot be reduced to atomic values; it requires interconnected entities
* *Recursive relationships* — a user has many sessions; a session has many exercises; each creates a nested, repeating structure
* *Temporal modeling* — timestamps and date fields introduce a time-ordered dimension to the data
* *Multi-dimensional systems* — the workout history system is an example of a domain that requires multiple axes to model correctly

The workout tracking system demonstrates how software requirements often involve highly structured and interconnected domain relationships that cannot be captured with simple flat data models.

